body:
@notatallshaw Thanks for the detailed response (and also for providing the solution quickly, I appreciate terseness!).

I checked a bit around, and I think this style of pinning to `<X.0.0a0` is something we took over from our conda packages (the conda-forge docs have this) into our pypi dependency specifications.

Replaced now with just pinning to `X.0.0`

@DEKHTIARJonathan I am supportive of variants in general, and ones like blas, openmp, cuda version, etc. should work fine because they are known and constrained ahead of time.

The issue here is resolving a variant marker that itself is dependent on another Python package version, where that Python package version is itself being resolved. My intuitive sense of them problem is that a solution must involve constraining the version of that Python package **ahead** of the resolution, i.e. if you want to say something like `"abi::torch::2.8.0" in variant_properties` you must have already constrained `pytorch==2.8.0` before the resolution began.

I don't know how else an advanced solver can support this, obviously you could write a solver that just brute forces the problem and was willing to run to the heat death of the universe for a solution. But if someone wants to show me a resolver in a real ecosystem that implements this kind of feature, and is able to implement a CDCL style or other advanced solver, I am happy to learn.

@notatallshaw 

> I am supportive of variants in general, and ones like blas, openmp, cuda version, etc. should work fine because they are known and constrained ahead of time.

So yes in general - **except** that more and more people package system libraries like blas, openmp, etc. as python packages ...

@wywarren  also something seems quite broken about your environment, e.g.

> urllib3                  None

The [published versions](https://pypi.org/project/urllib3/#history) of urllib3 have never had an invalid version, so something is installing these packages with invalid metadata, could be conda or linux package manager or another package manager. Pip needs valid metadata to be able to work, otherwise you need to stick with whatever package manager you are using to install these packages, that is able to operate with invalid metadata.

@ewdurbin thank you for fixing it!

Is it possible to call the bootstrap automation workflow whenever the upstream repositories are updated? I haven't worked with reusable GHA workflows so I'm not sure if there are limitations that would prevent us from using it, but it seems like a good way to avoid similar breakage in the future.

https://docs.github.com/en/actions/how-tos/reuse-automations/reuse-workflows

While I've seen a small number of projects pin to pip 25.2 by in large this appears to have been a fairly quiet release.

I'd like to keep main relatively frozen for the rest of the weekend, but after that I will consider 25.3 done, if an emergency comes up after that I will cherry pick the commits for a 25.3.1.

@pfmoore I've raised the PR to bundle pip into CPython: https://github.com/python/cpython/pull/140876

@ichard26
my guess is `pip install -e "pkg@ git+file://..."` just wasn't supported until #13495

might not be OS-related at all.

@alexanderankin `pip install -e[dev] -r pylock.toml` in #13876  should roughly do what you want. You are welcome to try it out and give feedback.

>  potentially looking for pylock.${platform}-${os}-${arch}.toml first, to address platform/os stuff. 

At this stage, I think pip should not try to select a specific pylock file based on the platform as such as naming scheme is not standardized, and pylock.toml standard has mechanism to support multiple platforms in a single lock file.
